  "text": "Current physical models, whether they describe everyday phenomena or quantum behavior, run into serious problems because some mathematical sums become infinite. A well‑known example is the infinite energy that a point‑like charged particle would possess due to its own field. Simple fixes, like assuming the particle has a small but finite size, break the requirement that the laws look the same for all observers moving at constant speeds, so they cannot be accepted. More elaborate frameworks that keep this requirement can remove some or all of the infinities, but they become highly complex and often leave us unsure how to interpret the results in physical terms."
